# ACF PHP Field Groups

Use this Rule when creating or changing any ACF field definition: Section field groups, Flexible Content layouts, CPT field groups, or shared `[TMPL]` clone-source groups.

## Definitions Live In PHP

- All ACF field definitions are PHP registrations under `themes/<child-theme>/smart-template-builder/acf/field-groups/` (served as `wp-content/themes/<child-theme>/smart-template-builder/acf/field-groups/`).
- One file per field group; register with `acf_add_local_field_group()` on the `acf/init` hook through the project's field-group loader.
- The Page-Builder Flexible Content container and all of its layouts are registered in PHP together. ACF cannot add PHP-registered fields to a Flexible Content field created in the admin, so mixing origins is not viable: each project performs a one-time migration of the admin-created Flexible Content group plus the shared TMPL groups into PHP before new PHP-only Sections are added.

## Keys And Names

- Every group, field, and layout carries a stable explicit key (`group_<unique>`, `field_<unique>`); generate a fresh unique key for every new definition.
- Never reuse an existing key for a different field, and never rename a saved field's `name` or `key` casually — stored content references both. Treat key or name changes as data migrations requiring an explicit user decision.
- Shared TMPL groups are PHP-registered as well; clone fields reference the shared group keys.

## No Structural Database Writes

Field definitions are code, not database records. The legacy hazard where a looped field-definition write-back destroyed shared clone-source definitions site-wide is structurally gone — and stays gone:

- Never create, update, reorder, or delete `acf-field` or `acf-field-group` posts programmatically: no `acf_update_field()` on definitions, no direct `$wpdb` writes to field-definition posts, no import scripts against the database.
- Definition changes happen only by editing the PHP files, committing, handing over for push, and verifying the deploy over the status bridge.
- Editing page or post *values* through the ACF content API is a separate concern and is not restricted by this Rule.

## Admin And Verification

- PHP-registered groups are not editable in the ACF admin; the admin is a read surface for definitions. Content values are still edited in the admin as usual.
- After a bridge-verified deploy: confirm that `GET /status` lists the expected ACF field groups with PHP origin, that the layout appears in the editor, that field values save and load, and that the frontend renders the Section.

This Rule never stores project field keys, group keys, layout keys, or field post IDs. Concrete keys live in the project repository and its `docs/` layer.
